---
name: coding-product-partner
description: Clarify product direction and user journeys, challenge assumptions, and shape narrow outcomes while owning routine decisions.
---

# Product Partner

Work with the user to understand the problem and choose a useful outcome. A polished request or a feasible implementation is not necessarily a complete brief. Use this in greenfield and brownfield work; depth follows uncertainty and consequence, not project age.

## Discovery And Autonomy

Product discovery is a conversation about where the product should go and what work it should make possible. Read enough relevant context to orient that conversation, then raise high-level questions before settling dependent specifications or implementation. Do not postpone the dialogue until an exhaustive investigation is finished. Discovery and specification/proof design do not start a native Goal; [Implement](../coding-implement/SKILL.md#start-the-goal) owns it once delivery is ready.

- Explore the intended users, their work, the product's role and ambition, and meaningful alternatives where these are unclear. Even a small request can expose a larger direction worth discussing; reuse established vision instead of reopening it for every change. Future possibilities inform today's choices without authorizing extra features.
- Bring a reasoned proposal into the discussion: how you would proceed, why, the strongest relevant alternatives, and their practical problems, limitations, and tradeoffs. Ask for missing context you cannot establish yourself and use the answers to compare directions with the user. When information is incomplete, offer a provisional recommendation and explain what would change it. A generic “shall I proceed?” adds no discovery.
- Own routine decisions. Derive fields, screen placement, storage representation, defaults, and implementation tactics from the agreed journey, existing conventions, and evidence. Do not turn each detail into a user choice or send a stream of optional preference questions. When a detail exposes a real business distinction, ask about the user's work and its consequences rather than asking them to design the schema.
- For clear authorized work and reversible choices within its scope, choose a supported direction, state material assumptions, and proceed. Record autonomous choices as assumptions, never as the user's answers or approval; keep optional suggestions out of the critical path.
- Wait only for work that depends on a consequential decision you cannot resolve from context or responsibly make within existing authority, such as expanding scope, changing access to private data, or an irreversible migration. Explain the concrete consequence, recommend an option, and continue independent authorized work. Silence or elapsed time cannot grant missing authority.
- Incorporate answers and corrections as they arrive, revisiting only affected decisions. Do not repeat pending questions or unchanged waiting notices. A skill transition introduces no new approval requirement.

For example, a CRM request may call for discussing whether the product is a lightweight companion to existing tools or the future home of the commercial workflow, and what users need to accomplish first. Tab names and individual field arrangements follow from that direction. An authorized visual refresh can reuse the existing layout and interactions. Changing who can see customer records still needs an explicit decision when the request and context do not establish that authority.

Known-behavior repairs and diagnosis-only work keep their existing routes. Return here when investigation reveals a new product decision, without restarting settled discovery.

## Discover And Decide

Inspect the request, relevant dialogue, repository behavior, existing decisions, and supplied references. Code establishes present capability and reuse opportunities, not the ceiling of the user's ambition. Explain the current understanding and a recommended direction before turning a brief into contracts. Keep exploration conversational until a decision is worth recording.

Ground the next slice in a concrete situation: the user's actual starting state, ordinary action, and useful result at the consumer. Investigate the assumption most likely to make that journey fail before dependent acceptance. Use available source, references, or a bounded authorized probe; incomplete documentation alone does not establish that a capability is missing. Separate what the user decided, what evidence establishes, and what remains assumed. Keep unavailable evidence as a limit and continue independent work. Scale investigation to the consequence; clear repairs need no additional discovery round.

Challenge a proposed solution when a materially different interpretation or simpler approach would better meet the need. Give the reason, practical consequence, and recommendation. Do not manufacture objections or treat every choice as unresolved.

- Resolve discoverable facts yourself. Ask for missing context about the user's work and choices with materially different product outcomes or consequences, rather than every choice that affects a screen or implementation.
- Group related questions early around the direction and journey, using concrete situations and meaningful alternatives. Questions can uncover the problem, test assumptions, or explore ambition; they are not limited to blockers. Do not require the user to fill in a technical questionnaire.
- After answers, revise the proposal and make the meaningful choices clear so the user can weigh them with you. Once the direction is established, recommend the first narrow outcome and take responsibility for its details. Follow up when new consequential uncertainty appears, not simply because another implementation detail has been discovered. An answer settles its own decision; announcing an interpretation is not agreement.
- Distinguish accepted decisions, recommendations, and assumptions. Infer reversible low-risk details when appropriate; do not freeze a consequential unsupported interpretation as accepted behavior.
- Keep questions focused and proportional. Once the next slice is sufficiently understood and authorized, carry it forward without a closing confirmation round. Reopen discovery when new evidence warrants it.

Select investigation lenses from the actual journey. For defaults or automation, inspect first use, absent configuration, eligibility, overrides, retries, and unavailable providers as relevant. For a usable application, trace entry/startup, real integrations, user actions, and visible saved results. Component success may leave that journey incomplete. Use a small visual comparison or authorized experiment when it will resolve uncertainty better than more prose; planning alone grants no implementation or new access.

## Carry Understanding Forward

Recommend one specific, demonstrable outcome for the next slice and keep a short view of how later slices compose into the user's journey. Keep necessary failure and permission behavior with the outcome; technical layers alone are not useful features. Later choices can remain provisional.

When the slice's outcome and authority are sufficiently established, use [Design Specs and Proof](../coding-design-specs-proof/SKILL.md) to turn that intent into narrow contracts and independent acceptance. Pass the original request/corrections and the concrete situation, with the evidence and material assumptions behind it. Preserve actual answers separately from recommendations; do not turn a proposed approach into user agreement. Exploration itself needs no feature package or mandatory APP/ARCHITECTURE scaffold.

A sufficiently clear request can continue to specification/proof design, and an already-ready feature to [Implement](../coding-implement/SKILL.md), while retaining the same critical attention to context and assumptions. Implementation starts its scoped Goal without renewed build approval. Analysis or specification-only work ends at its requested deliverable without a Goal. Partnership remains a stance throughout the work.
---
name: coding-design-specs-proof
description: Turn sufficiently understood product intent into narrow feature specifications and independent executable acceptance proof, preserving decisions and explicit assumptions.
---

# Design Specs and Proof

Translate understood intent into observable behavior and a realistic way to prove it. Apply [product discovery and autonomy](../coding-product-partner/SKILL.md#discovery-and-autonomy): establish the outcome, authority, and consequential decisions from the request, dialogue, and evidence. Return unresolved user-owned choices to Product Partner; a clear authorized slice needs no additional question-and-answer round before specification.

Read the original request, answers, existing contracts, and relevant repository evidence. Distinguish accepted decisions from recommendations and assumptions. Resolve discoverable engineering facts yourself; return material product uncertainty to [Product Partner](../coding-product-partner/SKILL.md#discover-and-decide). Continue settled work while independent questions remain pending.

Specification/proof design does not start a native Goal. Derive routine details from the established direction and evidence; do not reopen discovery for individual fields or technical choices. Specification-only scope ends at the requested deliverable and grants no implementation authority. [Implement](../coding-implement/SKILL.md#start-the-goal) starts the Goal when the next slice is ready for authorized implementation.

## Design Narrow Slices

Prefer one specific, independently demonstrable outcome per feature. Include the minimal end-to-end path and necessary failure/permission behavior. Split when outcomes can be accepted separately, decisions remain independent, or one uncertain integration would hold unrelated value hostage. Do not split merely by files or layers, or omit essential cases to make a spec look small.

For a broad request, maintain a short journey outline showing how slices connect, their dependencies, and deferred choices. Prepare the next useful slice in detail; keep later slices provisional until their decisions are ready. Do not require a complete backlog of executable proofs before delivering a settled slice. Continue toward every authorized outcome rather than quietly dropping later scope.

For example, order search, saved filters, and scheduled exports can be separate outcomes. The selected order export still needs its real download and correct contents; separate “API,” “button,” and “test” features would not each deliver that outcome. Refactors use a narrow responsibility with explicit preserved invariants.

## Record Earned Decisions

Place durable content by what it describes. General app descriptions, vision, journey overviews, and broader future direction belong in `app.md` or equivalent product documentation. System structure, visual architecture, and cross-feature technical decisions belong in `architecture.md` or equivalent architecture documentation. Feature-specific outcomes, contracts, and proof belong in their owning package under `docs/features/`, including drafts with open decisions. Keep future possibilities clearly provisional wherever they are recorded.

Reuse existing product and architecture documents and their naming conventions; create them only when durable context needs a home. Neither filename is a universal prerequisite. Update the relevant document or feature package when reviewing decisions, so the result stays with the content it explains. Add conventions/testing guidance only when it prevents repeated decisions.

Use one directory per feature at `docs/features/<feature-id>/`, with a stable lowercase hyphenated identifier. Reuse the owning directory when refining an existing feature. Use these exact filenames and paths:

```text
docs/features/
├── status.json                # shared lifecycle index
└── <feature-id>/
    ├── FEATURE.md             # outcome and behavior contract
    ├── PROOF.md               # acceptance contract and scenario mapping
    └── proof/
        ├── run.sh             # executable acceptance runner
        └── runs/              # retained execution evidence, created on runs
```

A durable draft requires a nonempty `FEATURE.md`; add `PROOF.md` and executable `proof/run.sh` before marking it ready. Do not create empty proof files or run directories merely to fill the tree. Keep acceptance tests and determining fixtures/helpers under `proof/`, documenting framework-required alternative paths in `PROOF.md`. Create the package files before registering the draft; the status helper records and validates them, rather than scaffolding them. Follow [status mechanics](../../docs/harness/coding/status.md) for index changes.

Record each settled slice in its `FEATURE.md`: intended outcome, observable behavior, material decisions, assumptions, boundaries, dependencies, and compatibility. Connect decisions to the request, actual answers, or supporting context; distinguish agent assumptions from user agreement and never invent a question-and-answer record. Use schemas only where they resolve ambiguity; keep commands and fixtures in proof. Link the whole journey when useful. Drafts may retain open decisions; only implementation dependent on an unresolved consequential user decision waits.

## Prepare Independent Acceptance

- Register durable drafts using [status mechanics](../../docs/harness/coding/status.md). Exploration itself needs no package.
- Supply the independent author with FEATURE, original intent/corrections, relevant context, and the named consumption target under the [proof contract](../../docs/harness/coding/proof.md). Use its [role briefs and examples](../../docs/harness/coding/proof-guidance.md) when designing an unfamiliar boundary. This skill does not merge implementer, acceptance-author, and final-reviewer responsibilities.
- Resolve pivotal engineering assumptions using available source or bounded authorized probes before freezing dependent acceptance. A provider fake must follow an evidenced contract; writing it cannot establish the capability it assumes. Retain the concrete starting state, action, consumer result, and unavailable boundary from discovery in the existing feature/proof, without another handoff document.
- Reconcile scenarios and proof with the original outcome. Resolve missing decisions rather than narrow the promise. Author setup checks establish that the proof machinery works; application assertions may correctly remain red.
- Mark a slice ready when its outcome and authority are clear, consequential user-owned decisions are resolved, remaining assumptions are justified, and its independent proof is executable. This is an accountable judgment, not a document-count test or another build-approval round.
- Retain prior evidence on interruption or revision. During delivery, acceptance repairs follow the proof contract; routine setup repair does not reopen product discovery.

Specification-only work ends at the requested deliverable without a Goal. An authorized build continues through [Implement](../coding-implement/SKILL.md), which starts or resumes its implementation Goal without renewed approval. Later slices can be prepared as needed; this skill is not a compulsory stop for small repairs or already-ready work.
# Shared Proof Contract

Acceptance rules for Design Specs and Proof, Implement, and feature-owned Repair. [AGENTS.md](../../../AGENTS.md) owns authorization.

Choose technique and depth for the accepted slice. Use [role briefs and boundary examples](proof-guidance.md) when helpful; they introduce no additional stage, quota, or approval.

## Separate Authorship

- Use a separate author before implementation. Supply FEATURE, dialogue/corrections, app context, and target.
- Apply [product discovery and autonomy](../../../skills/coding-product-partner/SKILL.md#discovery-and-autonomy): ground acceptance in the request, actual decisions, and justified explicit assumptions. Return unresolved consequential user choices before freezing dependent acceptance; no extra discovery round is required for sufficiently clear authorized behavior.
- The author writes PROOF, dedicated tests, and executable `proof/run.sh`.
- The author may inspect code and reuse utilities; it cannot implement behavior or edit FEATURE.
- Derive the starting state, ordinary action, and consumer result from the original request/corrections before reconciling the proposed FEATURE. Investigate disagreement instead of letting the specification and fixtures validate the same unsupported assumption. Return product gaps to Product Partner; never narrow the promise silently.
- An unavailable author does not permit implementer-authored acceptance.
- Keep acceptance under feature `proof/`; record framework-required alternative paths.
- Map scenarios to tests and all determining fixtures/helpers/configuration.
- Keep implementer regressions separate. Never import another feature's whole proof or relabel generic tests.

## Required Boundary

- Name input/producer, public activation, state owner, consumer, read-back, target, and external edge.
- Make bypassing the central behavior fail. Fake only unsafe/unavailable outer edges, never claimed behavior.
- Select relevant starting states, restart, errors, retries, concurrency, permissions, and provider variability.
- Carry accepted discovery cases into assertions.
- Prefilled fixtures alone cannot prove first-use defaults.
- Map plausible incomplete implementations to failing assertions. Uncovered material risks remain gaps.
- Reuse infrastructure. Reduce redundant machinery, not accepted coverage.
- At authoring and final review, ask: **Which promised ordinary action could fail while this proof passes?**
- Exercise the real entry/launch mode. Resolve material response-time expectations through Product Partner.
- Report measured limits without guessing causes. A generous timeout does not establish usability.
- UI: real browser/user actions and loaded, decoded, visible resources; no handler calls or dispatched DOM events.
- Domain effects: normal protected API plus read-back. Label presentation-only scope.
- API/worker/CLI: actual route/process plus observable results.
- Judge semantic outcomes across paraphrases, not exact wording.
- Gates, builds, source inspection, unobserved writes, and assistant claims are not acceptance.
- Source success is intermediate for live targets.

## Contract

PROOF records:

- Done, Command, environment/target, and evidence method.
- Scenario/test mapping: activation, consumer, read-back, fake, defect caught.
- Proves/Does not prove, false-green risks, and gaps.
- Deterministic assertions, sampled evidence, and unavailable live/manual checks.
- Case/repetition rationale and pass criteria for variable behavior; one sample cannot prove reliability.

Runner rules:

- Fail fast (`set -euo pipefail` unless deliberately aggregating).
- Emit non-secret readiness facts. Never edit application/acceptance, daemonize, or escape capture.
- Before expensive runs, the author checks changed helpers, readiness, fault synchronization, browser events, and cleanup as applicable.
- Reuse short checks; no mandatory preflight framework. Syntax or missing-environment exits do not prove runnable scenarios.
- Substitutes validate machinery only. Keep unavailable integration checks as prerequisites.
- Repeat machinery checks only for relevant changes or failures.

Blocked setup:

- Inspect docs, local tools, and authorized access first.
- Name the prerequisite, blocked step, safe target, success read-back, owner, and unblocking action.
- Use established credential setup; never expose secrets. Record gaps in existing evidence.
- Respect pending questions and continue independent work.

## Fixed Acceptance

- Freeze FEATURE, PROOF, runner, tests, and all determining shared inputs before Implement.
- Compare inputs before verification/completion. Implementers edit application and separate regressions only.

### Decision Boundary

- **Setup defect:** independent author repairs between attempts. Keep FEATURE, PROOF, scenarios, assertions, outcomes, selection, target, and real/fake boundaries unchanged.
- **Technical contract error:** author may repair without asking when accepted intent is established. Preserve outcomes, coverage, assertion strength, target, observation independence, and real/fake boundaries.
- **Missing assertion:** author may add coverage for already-agreed outcomes. New product choices return to Product Partner.
- **Unresolved choice:** ask if behavior, scope, cost, permissions, meaningful risk, or accepted outcomes differ. Explain consequences; pause dependent work until answered.

Every repair:

- Ground it in retained contracts/dialogue and independent evidence, not candidate failure alone.
- Retain original failures/inputs, author, exact diff, rationale, and revised hashes.
- Keep setup repair records outside PROOF. Stay in the same feature/claim/Goal.
- Refreeze, rerun complete proof and affected checks, and obtain final review.
- An unavailable author pauses the repair.

Never:

- Infer the oracle from the candidate, relax requirements, skip assertions, or seed the tested result.
- Move claimed behavior into a fake or narrow the promise because a provider is unavailable.
- Treat read-only calls as authorization for broader credentials.
- Require approval merely because a frozen file changes, or bypass boundaries by switching modes.

After an agreed consequential revision, align FEATURE/Goal and have the author revise proof. Observed failure overrides a pass.

## Restore Unrunnable Proof

- Have a separate author restore required proof from retained contracts, history, and corrections.
- Existing verification authority covers restoration; preserve scenarios, assertions, outcomes, target, and real/fake boundaries.
- Document path mappings. Apply repair evidence and decision rules above.
- Run full captured restored proof and required review. Old passes remain history.

## Official Attempts

- Use [evidence capture](evidence.md#evidence-for-one-feature); keep regressions separate.
- Retain meaningful red, distinct failures, interruptions, and final green.
- A newer failed/unfinished official attempt invalidates an older PASS. Regressions never establish acceptance.
- Capture checks the recorded repository candidate and owning acceptance tree before/after execution, retaining scope and target. Include otherwise ignored determining inputs explicitly; see [capture limits](evidence.md#current-candidate-and-review).
- New completion requires current evidence and an attributed independent review receipt. Historical passes remain history, not current regression assurance.
- Hashes and role identifiers are audit evidence, not enforced write isolation, authentication, or external runtime attestation.
# Designing and challenging acceptance

Load the relevant brief or example when authoring or reviewing an unfamiliar proof boundary. These are techniques, not additional lifecycle stages; [proof.md](proof.md) owns the rules.

## Independent Author

Start from the accepted outcome and original user context. Identify how a real user or consumer activates it, where its authoritative result lives, and how a separate observation can contradict a false success. Inspect enough implementation to find those boundaries without deriving expected results from the candidate.

Choose the smallest realistic proof that would fail for a plausible incomplete implementation. Explain what it establishes and leaves unknown. Use established test infrastructure; do not build a bespoke framework when a process invocation, browser interaction, and read-back suffice. Exercise uncertain helpers before long runs. Return product ambiguity to Product Partner rather than choosing the easiest oracle.

Give the implementer the contract, runnable command, prerequisites, input versions, and relevant gaps. Preserve original failures and repair rationale. Independence is about who establishes acceptance and from which evidence, not merely a different agent name.

## Final Reviewer

Start with the original request/corrections and form an expectation of the ordinary user outcome before reading completion summaries and PASS results. Then inspect the accepted feature, changed surface, proof boundary, results, and repairs. Follow the highest-consequence shared assumption to independent source or consumer evidence; agreement between specification, fixture, and implementation is not corroboration. Challenge the completion claim against the tested candidate and target:

- Which ordinary promised action could still fail while every assertion passes?
- Did proof activate the real path and observe the authoritative result? Could a fake, prefilled state, stale build, or shared implementation assumption hide the defect?
- Did implementation or proof repair change the accepted meaning? Does a separate product choice remain unsupported?
- Are relevant regressions and the reviewed files current? Does the evidence establish local behavior, a live target, or only a narrower prerequisite?

Return a clear verdict with evidence-backed blockers and demonstrated limits. Keep stylistic preferences optional. Review stays read-only; the coordinator records the actual verdict and attribution, and re-verifies affected behavior after repairs. Passing output is evidence to assess, not a verdict to echo.

## Boundary Examples

| Promised outcome | Useful activation and independent read-back | False confidence to challenge |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Save a customer | Normal authenticated API or UI; reload and read the authoritative store | Handler returns success but transaction never commits |
| Provider delivery | Normal producer; authorized sandbox provider read-back and consumer result | Adapter returns an invented ID; local queue acceptance mistaken for delivery |
| Worker processing | Start the actual worker, submit input, observe durable result and relevant retry behavior | Calling an internal function bypasses scheduling, configuration, or persistence |
| Local startup | Run the documented default command in its actual terminal/process mode; check readiness and shutdown | Detached-only smoke test misses an attached-terminal failure |
| UI feature | Real browser actions, persisted result after reload, appropriate permissions and failure state | Screenshot or direct handler invocation skips actual interaction |
| Generated report/export | Invoke normal generation/download and inspect meaningful content and format | File existence or HTTP 200 accepts empty or incorrect output |
| First-use automation | Begin without saved preferences and observe a useful permitted result | Prefilled fixtures hide the missing selection or fallback policy |
| Refactor | Exercise the preserved public contract or invariant through affected consumers | Snapshot of private structure rewards the rewrite without checking equivalence |
| Harness instruction change | Fresh task execution with raw context and withheld expectations | Retained text judgments or heading checks presented as actual agent behavior |

Select relevant error/recovery cases from the promise. These examples are neither a quota nor a reason to expand a narrow feature. For provider variability or sampled model behavior, explain the sampling limit rather than turning one pass into a reliability claim.

